INSTALLATION:

RPM or DEB. Note since FSlint does not need to be compiled
one can run it directly from the extracted source directory.
Please see the FAQ for details on building installation packages.

REQUIREMENTS:

FSlint 3 requires Python 3.9 or newer, PyGObject, and GTK 3.24.
The command-line scanners require GNU coreutils, findutils, and the
usual shell tools. Individual scanners also use file, grep, and other
utilities appropriate to their task.

On Fedora, install python3-gobject and gtk3. On Debian/Ubuntu, install
python3-gi and gir1.2-gtk-3.0. GTK 4 may be installed alongside GTK 3,
but this version explicitly loads the GTK 3 introspection namespace.
No Python 2, PyGTK, or libglade dependencies remain.

The GUI follows the desktop light/dark preference through the XDG
Settings portal, with GNOME settings as a fallback. Adwaita switches
variants automatically, including while the window is open. Custom
GTK themes retain their theme name. An explicit GTK_THEME environment
variable overrides automatic appearance selection.

DEVELOPMENT:

Run ./fslint-gui [PATHS] directly from the checkout.

The interface is fslint.ui, a standard GTK 3 GtkBuilder definition.
Open fslint.cmb in Cambalache 0.99 or newer, or create a GTK 3 project
and import fslint.ui in an older Cambalache release. Save UI changes
from Cambalache to fslint.ui; the runtime loads that file, not the
project file. Validate changes with:

    gtk-builder-tool validate fslint.ui

The .cmb project and .ui file have been round-tripped through
Cambalache 0.99.8. The result views are ordinary GtkTreeView widgets;
fslint_gtk.py supplies their runtime models and file metadata. Keep
the model column attributes in sync if you change the view columns.
New widget signals need matching handlers in fslint-gui.

This migration targets GTK 3 to retain the existing workflows.
A future GTK 4 port requires changes to menus, synchronous dialogs,
event handling, and result views; GTK 4 alone is not sufficient to
run this release.

Run migration regression checks with:

    python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v

GUI tests need an available display and otherwise skip. For a headless
machine with Xvfb installed, use:

    xvfb-run -a python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v

Tests use temporary directories for scans and file actions. Package
listing is read-only; the tests do not uninstall packages. Existing
scanner restrictions, including newline exclusions in some modes,
remain in effect.

DESCRIPTION:

FSlint is a toolkit to find redundant/questionable disk usage.
The fslint-gui command invokes the GUI interface. There is also
a command line interface to each tool which can be accessed
from the /usr/share/fslint/fslint directory usually.
The command fslint is a wrapper for the various
tools which can be called individually. All the tools
(including fslint) have the same interface, which is
basically:

command [-r] [-f] [path1 [path2]...]

where the paths are files &/or directories
If no paths are specified then the current
directory is assumed.

-r means do NOT recurse the specified director{y,ies}
-f means output full paths even if only 1 path specified

some tools have their own options. To get help on
each tool do:

command --help

Any extra commands are passed to find, so any of
filtering commands will work, for e.g the following
means process the whole system but do not traverse
onto any other mounted filesystems:

fslint / -xdev

More examples:

	search for duplicates in current directory and below
		findup or findup .
	same as above but don't look in subdirectories
		findup -r .
	search for duplicate names in /usr/bin
		findsn /usr/bin
	search in multiple directories but not their subdirectories
		findsn -r /usr/bin /bin /usr/sbin /sbin
	search system for duplicate files over 100K in size
		findup / -size +100k
	search only my files (that I own and are in my home dir)
		findsn ~ -user `id -u`
	search system for duplicate files names belonging to "root"
		findsn / -user `id -u root`

Note the fslint tools do a lot of inode access and to speed them
up you can use the following method to not update access times
on disk while gathering inode information:
mount -o remount,noatime mountpoint
fslint
mount -o atime,remount mountpoint
